-module-scss-module__X6FS6a__content"><p data-block-key=3D"nae94"><i>NASA d=
oes not endorse any non-U.S. Government entity and is not responsible for i=
nformation contained on non-U.S. Government websites. For non-U.S. Governme=
nt websites, participants must comply with any data use parameters of that =
specific website.</i></p><h2 data-block-key=3D"dd7f5"><b>NASA Data &amp; Re=
sources</b></h2><p data-block-key=3D"690hn"><a href=3D"https://www.earthdat=
a.nasa.gov/data/platforms/space-based-platforms/smap"><b>Soil Moisture Acti=
ve Passive (SMAP)</b></a><b>:</b> SMAP provides global measurements of surf=
ace soil moisture and freeze/thaw state, collected every 2=E2=80=933 days. =
Soil=E2=80=91moisture information helps identify drought stress, water avai=
lability, infiltration potential, and early crop=E2=80=91stress signals. Th=
ese data are valuable for evaluating rotation crops that could improve wate=
r efficiency, soil conditions, or drought resilience.</p><p data-block-key=
=3D"f0o41"><a href=3D"https://gpm.nasa.gov/data"><b>Global Precipitation Me=
asurement (GPM) &amp; IMERG</b></a><b>:</b> The GPM mission and its IMERG p=
roduct provide global precipitation rate and accumulation estimates. These =
help assess rainfall reliability, extreme=E2=80=91storm frequency, erosion =
risk, water input to soils, and long=E2=80=91term trends in field moisture.=
 IMERG is valuable for comparing rotation strategies under different precip=
itation scenarios. <i>(Worldview IMERG layers can be downloaded directly as=
 high=E2=80=91resolution images.)</i></p><p data-block-key=3D"8hdc0"><a hre=
f=3D"https://earthexplorer.usgs.gov/" data-external=3D"" data-processed=3D"=
true"><b>Landsat</b><span class=3D"icon-container"><svg class=3D"icon icon-=
module-scss-module__Ik2gAa__icon icon-module-scss-module__Ik2gAa__stroke un=
defined "><use href=3D"/assets/sprite.svg#spaceapps/external"></use></svg><=
/span></a><b> and</b> <a href=3D"https://lpdaac.usgs.gov/products/" data-ex=
ternal=3D"" data-processed=3D"true"><b>MODIS</b><span class=3D"icon-contain=
er"><svg class=3D"icon icon-module-scss-module__Ik2gAa__icon icon-module-sc=
ss-module__Ik2gAa__stroke undefined "><use href=3D"/assets/sprite.svg#space=
apps/external"></use></svg></span></a><b>/</b><a href=3D"https://search.ear=
thdata.nasa.gov/search?q=3DVIIRS"><b>VIIRS</b></a><b>: Reflectance, NDVI, V=
egetation Indices:</b> Landsat (30=E2=80=91meter resolution) and MODIS/VIIR=
S (250=E2=80=931000 m) provide corrected reflectance, NDVI, vegetation indi=
ces, and indicators of plant stress, bare ground, and seasonal change. Thes=
e datasets allow participants to map crop vigor, track seasonal transitions=
, detect high=E2=80=91stress periods, and compare rotation options visually=
.</p><p data-block-key=3D"7qbmd"><a href=3D"https://power.larc.nasa.gov/"><=
b>NASA POWER</b></a><b>: Agroclimatology &amp; Meteorological Data:</b> NAS=
A POWER provides accessible solar, meteorological, and climatology datasets=
 used widely in agriculture. The datasets include temperature, humidity, ra=
diation, and derived indicators that help estimate crop water use, growing=
=E2=80=91season conditions, frost risk, and climate suitability for potenti=
al rotation crops.</p><p data-block-key=3D"e2nhr"><a href=3D"https://www.ea=
rthdata.nasa.gov/data/instruments/srtm"><b>Shuttle Radar Topography Mission=
 (SRTM)</b></a><b>: Elevation &amp; Terrain Models:</b> The Shuttle Radar T=
opography Mission (SRTM) provides global elevation data suitable for analyz=
ing slope, runoff, erosion risk, water flow, and terrain=E2=80=91driven str=
ess conditions. Terrain patterns help determine which rotation crops may im=
prove infiltration, reduce erosion, or stabilize soil on slopes.</p><p data=
-block-key=3D"682qe"><a href=3D"https://ecostress.jpl.nasa.gov/data"><b>ECO=
STRESS</b></a><b>:</b> ECOSTRESS measures land=E2=80=91surface temperature =
(LST) and evapotranspiration (ET) at high spatial resolution. This helps de=
tect hotspots of plant stress, heat exposure, shallow=E2=80=91root vulnerab=
ility, and crop=E2=80=91water demand. It is particularly useful for identif=
ying when current crops may be becoming more vulnerable and for mapping fie=
ld=E2=80=91level microclimate differences.</p><p data-block-key=3D"72rjc"><=
a href=3D"https://www.nal.usda.gov/services/agdatacommons" data-external=3D=
